BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:david-krueger@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20250909T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20250909T180000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — David Krueger (University of Montreal)
DESCRIPTION:Krueger gave a whirlwind tour of AI safety as a set of concer
 ns\, concepts and communities — the history of the field\, his own pat
 h through it\, and the case that academics across disciplines can and sh
 ould help shape where AI goes next.\n\nZoom: https://berkeley.zoom.us/j/
 96396540012\n\nRecording: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NX2YROCIJNQ\n\
 nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nikola-jurkovic@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20250923T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20250923T180000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Nikola Jurkovic (METR)
DESCRIPTION:Jurkovic walked through METR's work evaluating frontier model
 s\, using their GPT-5 evaluation as a case study\, and asked what a cred
 ible safety case for a frontier model should actually look like.\n\nZoom
 : https://berkeley.zoom.us/j/96396540012\n\nRecording: https://www.youtu
 be.com/watch?v=oiM5ZUg22UI\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bharat-chandar@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251007T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251007T180000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Bharat Chandar (Stanford University)
DESCRIPTION:Drawing on payroll records from the largest US payroll provid
 er\, Chandar presented evidence that early-career workers in the occupat
 ions most exposed to generative AI have seen a 13 percent relative decli
 ne in employment — while older and less-exposed workers have not.\n\nZ
 oom: https://berkeley.zoom.us/j/96396540012\n\nRecording: https://www.yo
 utube.com/watch?v=g6kV_6yqkJQ\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stuart-russell@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251021T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251021T180000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Stuart Russell (UC Berkeley)
DESCRIPTION:Russell argued that Turing was right to worry about losing co
 ntrol of machines that exceed human capabilities\, but wrong to treat do
 om as inevitable — and set out the technical and regulatory directions
  he believes lead somewhere better.\n\nZoom: https://berkeley.zoom.us/j/
 96396540012\n\nRecording: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9ITf6JilEAc\n\
 nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jessica-newman@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251118T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251118T180000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Jessica Newman (UC Berkeley)
DESCRIPTION:Newman measured the fast-moving AI governance landscape — t
 he General-Purpose AI Code of Practice\, a dozen-plus corporate frontier
  safety frameworks — against the risk picture shifting underneath it\,
  and named the priorities she thinks are being missed.\n\nZoom: https://
 berkeley.zoom.us/j/96396540012\n\nRecording: https://www.youtube.com/wat
 ch?v=WHJK-NfWXCY\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:Zoom only
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:deborah-raji@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251202T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251202T180000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Deborah Raji (UC Berkeley)
DESCRIPTION:Raji argued that most working definitions of “AI safety” 
 fail to capture the sociotechnical nature of these systems\, and so mana
 ge to overstate some risks while understating others.\n\nZoom: https://b
 erkeley.zoom.us/j/96396540012\n\nRecording: https://www.youtube.com/watc
 h?v=Hh0BowuitF0\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:holly-elmore@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251209T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20251209T180000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Holly Elmore (PauseAI US)
DESCRIPTION:Elmore traced PauseAI's theory of change back to her own trai
 ning in evolutionary biology\, arguing that arbitrary intelligence means
  arbitrary power\, and that the resulting disruption is a social problem
  rather than a bug awaiting a fix.\n\nZoom: https://berkeley.zoom.us/j/9
 6396540012\n\nRecording: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RaBQfgQY6JU\n\n
 https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:deirdre-mulligan@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260203T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260203T173000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Deirdre Mulligan (UC Berkeley)
DESCRIPTION:Mulligan described three competing imaginaries of AI — resp
 onsible\, frontier and pragmatic — and showed how each one quietly pre
 configures the regulation that follows it. Joint work with Victor Z. Wan
 g.\n\nZoom: https://berkeley.zoom.us/j/96396540012\n\nRecording: https:/
 /www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ax1-SzEpDPs\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:luke-drago@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260217T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260217T173000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Luke Drago (Workshop Labs)
DESCRIPTION:Drago argued that labor displacement\, not abstract risk\, wi
 ll be what finally drives political action on AI — and that the window
  between visible displacement and a sudden capability takeoff is the bes
 t chance anyone gets to shape what is built.\n\nZoom: https://berkeley.z
 oom.us/j/96396540012\n\nRecording: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=plJz1
 LtWwIU\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:helen-toner@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260305T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260305T183000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Helen Toner (Center for Security and Emergin
 g Technology\, Georgetown University)
DESCRIPTION:In conversation with Berkeley Law's Rachel Stern\, Toner test
 ed the stories that circulate about Chinese AI — the race\, the export
  controls\, the case for cooperation — against the available evidence.
 \n\nZoom: https://berkeley.zoom.us/j/96396540012\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig
 .ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:306 Soda Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jacob-steinhardt@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260317T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260317T173000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Jacob Steinhardt (UC Berkeley)
DESCRIPTION:Steinhardt made the case for scalable measurement as the leve
 r that shapes AI development\, covering scalable oversight of agent tran
 scripts\, discovery of long-tail behaviors\, and explanation of model in
 ternals — using AI to understand AI.\n\nZoom: https://berkeley.zoom.us
 /j/96396540012\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:chad-jones@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260414T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260414T173000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Chad Jones (Stanford University)
DESCRIPTION:Jones asked what economics has to say if machines can eventua
 lly perform every task a human can\, more cheaply: whether AI is simply 
 the next general purpose technology\, or something categorically differe
 nt.\n\nZoom: https://berkeley.zoom.us/j/96396540012\n\nhttps://www.evanb
 rig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:allison-duettmann@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260428T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260428T173000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Allison Duettmann (Foresight Institute)
DESCRIPTION:Duettmann argued that AI safety research is over-concentrated
  — geographically\, institutionally and intellectually — and present
 ed Foresight's AI Nodes as a deliberately distributed alternative.\n\nZo
 om: https://berkeley.zoom.us/j/96396540012\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BA
 IRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nate-soares@ai-risk.berkeley.edu
DTSTAMP:20260910T070522Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260908T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260908T173000
SUMMARY:Berkeley AI Risk — Nate Soares (MIRI)
DESCRIPTION:Drawing on his book with Eliezer Yudkowsky\, Soares argued th
 at the current trajectory toward superintelligence ends predictably in h
 uman extinction — not through malice but through indifference — and 
 that there is still time to change course.\n\nZoom: https://berkeley.zoo
 m.us/j/96396540012\n\nhttps://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
LOCATION:621 Sutardja Dai Hall
URL:https://www.evanbrig.ht/BAIRS/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
